    "summary": "Sweet Dark Star, tight Playing in the Band, soaring Sugar Magnolia",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a luscious Dark Star → Eyes Of The World → Stella Blue suite and a fiery Playing In The Band jam. Jerry’s vocals are expressive and his solos soar, while Keith’s Rhodes adds shimmering texture. Highlights include Bird Song, Black Peter (one‑drummer) and an upbeat Sugar Magnolia.",
    "review": "June 30 1973 at the Universal Amphitheatre captures the Dead in a classic mid‑summer groove. The first set rolls through tight country‑rock numbers before launching into a second‑set masterpiece: Dark Star segues seamlessly into Eyes Of The World and then into Stella Blue, a sequence praised across every recording for its fluid transitions and exploratory improvisation. Jerry Garcia’s voice is strong and his wah‑wah‑laden solos on Dark Star and Cumberland Blues are especially emotive, while Phil Lesh lays down deep, warm bass that anchors the jams. Keith Godchaux’s Rhodes piano glitters throughout, adding a delicate counterpoint to the guitars, and Bill Kreutzmann provides crisp, driving rhythms that keep the energy high. Standout moments include a soaring Playing In The Band, an inspired Bird Song, a rare one‑drummer Black Peter, and a bright Sugar Magnolia that closes the night on a jubilant note. Across matrix, soundboard, and audience sources the performance shines, making this show a must‑listen for any 1973 Dead fan.",
